6 The arithmetic progression version of the conjecture
The formally strongest version of the Strong(α,β)-Divisor Conjecture is that there are
arithmetic progressionsAof cardinalityn 2β, whose elements have magnitudeexp(nα), that
satisfy then-divisor property. We call this the “Arithmetic Progression Version” of the
conjecture. Proposition 3.4 shows that the Arithmetic Progression Version implies the Strong
(α,β)-Divisor Conjecture. Thus, if one is to disprove a version of the conjecture, the easiest
version to attack is the arithmetic progression one. Here we make some observations that
may be useful in studying this version.
First, recall that such an arithmetic progressionA={b+ic: 1≤i≤n 2β}satisfying the
n-Divisor Property yields an improved exponent for polynomial factorization ifα,β <1/2.
One observation is thatb̸= 0is necessary for non-triviality. For ifb= 0then we have that
every primep < ndividesicfor somei≤n 2β, and this requirescto be the product of all
primes betweenn 2β andn, which is a number of magnitudeexp(n−o(1))whenβ <1/2.
It is also necessary that(b+c)≥exp(n 1−2β)as we have argued in Section 3. Although it
seems unlikely, we cannot rule out the possibility that the Arithmetic Progression Version
may hold withb≤exp(O(n 1−2β))andc= 1!
Certainsubsetsof integers in[n]are easy to handle: for example, by choosingcto be the
least common multiple of all positive integers of magnitude at mostnα, andb= 0, it is easy
to see that the arithmetic progressionA={b+ic: 1≤i≤n 2β}contains multiples of each
integer that can be writtenxywherex≤n 2β andy≤n α. On the other hand, some subsets
of integers seem difficult. Two challenging sets of integers are (1) the primes in[n], and (2)
compositesxywherex,yare in the range[n 1/2−ϵ,n 1/2]for a constantϵ>0.
A useful fact is that different arithmetic progressions containing multiples of different
subsets of integers can be combined into a single generalized arithmetic progression:
Lemma 6.1.LetA 1 ={b 1 +ic 1 : 1≤i≤ℓ 1}andA 2 ={b 2 +ic 2 : 1≤i≤ℓ 2}be two
arithmetic progressions. Then the generalized arithmetic progression
A={c 1b2 +i(c 1c2) +j(c 2b1−c 1b2) : 1≤i≤max(ℓ 1,ℓ 2),0≤j≤1}
contains multiples ofxifA 1 orA 2 does.
Proof.Supposexdividesb 1 +ic 1 for somei. Then the(i,1)term inA, which equals
c2b1 +i(c 1c2) =c 2(b1 +ic 1)is also divisible byx. Similarly, ifxdividesb 2 +ic 2 for somei,
then the(i,0)term inA, which equalsc 1b2 +i(c 1c2) =c 1(b2 +ic 2)is divisible byx.
